        {
          "unit_id": "good_news",
          "title": "Good News",
          "description": "Cuenta lo que dijo otra persona con el estilo indirecto: said, told, asked. Además, comparar cómo se hacen las cosas (more quickly, the hardest) e intensificadores (extremely, much too).",
          "lessons": [
            {
              "lesson_id": "good_news_lesson",
              "title": "Reported speech",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "phase": "context",
                  "type": "audio_context",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Good news",
                    "dialogue": [
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "You look excited. What did Helen want?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "She said that the museum loved our website!"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Brilliant! What else did she say?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "She told me that she wanted me to speak at a design conference in Edinburgh in October."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Speak? In front of hundreds of people?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "I know! I told her I was nervous, but she said I would be great."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "She's right. You'll be grand."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "She also asked if you could come and help with the technical part."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Me? In Edinburgh? Sounds good to me."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "And there's more news. Javi called this morning. He said he had passed his English exam!"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Fair play to him! What's he going to do now?"
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Lucía",
                        "text": "He told me he had got a job at a hotel in Dublin. He starts in September."
                      },
                      {
                        "speaker": "Daniel",
                        "text": "Dublin! My mam will want to feed him every Sunday."
                      }
                    ],
                    "passage": "July. Lucía runs into Daniel's office after a meeting with Helen."
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "discovery",
                  "type": "multiple_choice",
                  "content": {
                    "instructions": "Fíjate en el diálogo y responde.",
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "question": "\"She said that the museum loved our website.\" What did Helen actually say?",
                        "options": [
                          "\"The museum loves your website.\"",
                          "\"The museum loved my website.\""
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"She told me that…\" After told we need…",
                        "options": [
                          "a person (me, him…)",
                          "nothing"
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"She asked if you could come.\" Helen's question was…",
                        "options": [
                          "\"Can Daniel come?\"",
                          "\"Could Daniel came?\""
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      },
                      {
                        "question": "\"He said he had passed his exam.\" Javi's words were…",
                        "options": [
                          "\"I've passed my exam!\"",
                          "\"I pass my exam.\""
                        ],
                        "answer": 0
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "reported_speech",
                    "title": "Reported speech",
                    "explanation": "Para contar lo que alguien dijo usamos «say (that)» o «tell» + persona + «(that)»:\n- «She said that…» / «She told me that…»\n\nNormalmente el verbo da un paso atrás en el pasado:\n- presente → pasado («loves» → «loved»)\n- «will» → «would»\n- «can» → «could»\n- present perfect / past simple → past perfect («have passed» → «had passed»)\n\nLas preguntas de sí o no se cuentan con «asked if» / «whether» y orden de frase afirmativa (nunca «if could you come»):\n- «She asked if you could come.»\n\nCambian también los pronombres y los posesivos: «your website» → «our website».",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Lo que dijo",
                        "Estilo indirecto"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "\"The museum loves your website.\"",
                          "She said (that) the museum loved our website."
                        ],
                        [
                          "\"I want you to speak in Edinburgh.\"",
                          "She told me (that) she wanted me to speak in Edinburgh."
                        ],
                        [
                          "\"You'll be great.\"",
                          "She said I would be great."
                        ],
                        [
                          "\"Can Daniel come?\"",
                          "She asked if Daniel could come."
                        ],
                        [
                          "\"I've passed my exam!\"",
                          "He said he had passed his exam."
                        ],
                        [
                          "\"I'm nervous.\"",
                          "I told her I was nervous."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "She said that the museum loved our website!",
                      "I told her I was nervous, but she said I would be great.",
                      "She asked if you could come.",
                      "He told me he had got a job at a hotel in Dublin."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "adverbs_intensifiers",
                    "title": "Adverbs: more quickly, the hardest · extremely, much too",
                    "explanation": "Los adverbios también se comparan:\n- Con «-ly»: «more» / «the most» + adverbio: «She speaks more clearly than me.», «He works the most carefully.»\n- Adverbios cortos («fast», «hard», «late», «early»): «-er» / «the -est»: «Daniel works harder than anyone.», «Who arrived the earliest?»\n- Irregulares: «well» → «better» → «the best»; «badly» → «worse» → «the worst»: «Lucía speaks English better than last year.»\n\nPara reforzar o suavizar adjetivos y adverbios: «quite» (bastante), «really» / «very» (muy), «extremely» / «incredibly» (extremadamente), «much too» / «far too» (demasiado, con un matiz negativo):\n- «The museum was extremely happy.»\n- «The conference is much too big for me!»\n\n«So» + adjetivo o adverbio; «such» + («a») adjetivo + nombre:\n- «I was so nervous.»\n- «It's such good news!»",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "more + -ly",
                          "Helen explained it more clearly than me."
                        ],
                        [
                          "-er / -est",
                          "Daniel works harder than anyone in the office."
                        ],
                        [
                          "better / the best",
                          "You did the best presentation."
                        ],
                        [
                          "extremely",
                          "The client was extremely happy."
                        ],
                        [
                          "much too",
                          "Hundreds of people? That's much too many!"
                        ],
                        [
                          "so / such",
                          "It's such good news! I'm so happy for you."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Lucía speaks English much better than last year.",
                      "Daniel finished the website more quickly than everyone expected.",
                      "The museum was extremely pleased with the design.",
                      "Speaking in front of hundreds of people? That's much too scary!"
                    ]
                  }
                },
